// heartsim tutorial: the heart sounds and what the rhythm does to them. Original text by the heartsim
// authors, MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/tutorial.rs.
// The sounds are the model of briefs B24-B27; every clinical statement below carries its source in
// the references list.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "11_heart_sounds",
    title: "Heart sounds and the rhythm",
    summary: "A sound check, then S1 and S2 against the QRS and the T wave, the split and breathing, what the PR interval, bundle-branch block, complete block and atrial fibrillation do to the sounds, and the gallops S3 and S4.",
    references: [
        "Felner JM. The first heart sound. In: Walker HK, Hall WD, Hurst JW (eds). Clinical Methods, 3rd ed. Butterworths 1990, ch. 22. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333.",
        "Felner JM. The second heart sound. In: Clinical Methods, 3rd ed. Butterworths 1990, ch. 23. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK341.",
        "Williams ES. The fourth heart sound. In: Clinical Methods, 3rd ed. Butterworths 1990, ch. 25. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK344.",
        "MSD Manual Professional Version: Cardiac auscultation. msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/approach-to-the-cardiac-patient/cardiac-auscultation.",
        "Watsjold B, Ilgen J, Monteiro S et al. Do you hear what you see? Utilizing phonocardiography to enhance proficiency in cardiac auscultation. Perspect Med Educ 2021;10:148-154. doi:10.1007/s40037-020-00646-5.",
        "Tavel ME. Cardiac auscultation: a glorious past - and it does have a future! Circulation 2006;113:1255-1259. doi:10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.105.591149.",
    ],
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "Headphones and a sound check",
            markdown: r#"
Heart sounds are **low**: most of S1 and S2, and all of S3, S4 and the rumble of mitral stenosis,
lie below about 200 Hz. Laptop and phone speakers reproduce little down there, so **use
headphones**. Training modules that teach auscultation check this before they start (Watsjold et
al. 2021 played a 200 Hz tone); so does this one.

Play the two tones below. If you cannot hear the low one, your speakers are too small: put on
headphones, and follow every page with the **heart-sound lane** under the strip, where each sound
is drawn and labelled in time with the ECG. Everything in this tutorial can be done by eye.

The sounds you will hear are a **model** computed from the simulated beats, not a recording.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Overview, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SoundCheck),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "You cannot hear the 200 Hz tone. What should you do?",
                choices: [
                    "Use headphones, and follow the labelled heart-sound lane under the strip",
                    "Turn the volume to the maximum and carry on with the laptop speakers",
                    "Skip the tutorial: heart sounds cannot be learnt without sound",
                    "Listen at a different site",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Small speakers lose the low frequencies whatever the volume. Headphones bring them back; the lane shows every sound in time with the ECG, so the timing can be learnt by eye as well.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "S1 with the QRS, S2 at the end of the T wave",
            markdown: r#"
The heart sounds are made by valves closing, and the valves close because the ventricles contract
and relax, so each sound has its place against the ECG (Felner, ch. 22 and 23):

* **S1** - closure of the mitral (M1) and tricuspid (T1) valves - starts just after the **QRS**,
  as the ventricles begin to contract.
* **S2** - closure of the aortic (A2) and pulmonary (P2) valves - falls near the **end of the T
  wave**, as the ventricles relax.

At normal rates systole (S1 to S2) is shorter than diastole (S2 to the next S1): the **shorter
pause follows S1**. That is how to tell the two apart by ear. The sound is now on (where this
computer has a sound output) and you are listening at the apex.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Where does S2 fall on the ECG?",
                choices: ["Near the end of the T wave", "Just after the QRS", "On the P wave", "In the middle of the PR interval"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "S2 falls near the end of the T wave, when the ventricles relax and the aortic and pulmonary valves close. Just after the QRS is S1.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "The split of S2 follows the breath",
            markdown: r#"
At the **pulmonic area** (2nd intercostal space, left sternal border) S2 has two parts. In
**inspiration** more blood returns to the right heart, the right ventricle empties later and **P2
follows A2** by a few tens of milliseconds: the split is heard. In **expiration** the two close
together and S2 sounds single (Felner, ch. 23).

The patient is now **holding the breath at peak inspiration**, so every beat has the same, widest
split. Look at the lane: the labels read **A2·P2**. Then open the **Display** popup, under **Breathing**, and choose
**Hold at end-expiration**: the split closes. Choose **Breathing normally** to hear it come and go.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Pulmonic, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Sim(BreathHold(phase: Some(PeakInspiration))),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "In a healthy heart, when is S2 split?",
                choices: ["In inspiration", "In expiration", "Always, by the same amount", "Never"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Inspiration delays P2: the split opens on breathing in and closes on breathing out. A split that does not move with the breath, or opens in expiration, is abnormal.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Slow motion: the same sounds, longer gaps",
            markdown: r#"
A split of 40 ms is short. Halving or quartering the speed is an aid expert reviewers recommend
(Tavel 2006): in heartsim the **gaps between the sounds stretch** while **each sound keeps its
pitch**. It is an **altered** sound, labelled so on the lane, and no study has shown that it
improves learning, so come back to full speed once you can hear the split.

The speed is now **0.25×** and right bundle-branch block is loaded: P2 comes late in both phases of
the breath (a **wide split**). Listen at the pulmonic area, then set the speed back to 1.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("rbbb")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Pulmonic, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(0.25)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What does slow motion change in heartsim?",
                choices: [
                    "The gaps between the sounds stretch; each sound keeps its pitch",
                    "Every sound becomes lower in pitch",
                    "Nothing: the sound is the same as at full speed",
                    "It removes the murmurs",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The slow-motion sound is re-timed, not pitch-shifted: a 40 ms split at 0.25× is heard as 160 ms. The trace on the lane always shows the true timing.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A long PR softens S1",
            markdown: r#"
The loudness of S1 depends on where the mitral leaflets are when the ventricle contracts. After a
**short PR** they are still wide open and slam shut: **loud S1**. After a **long PR** they have
drifted almost closed: **soft S1** (Felner, ch. 22).

First-degree AV block is loaded (PR about 230 ms). Listen at the apex: S1 is now quieter than S2.
On the lane the S1 envelope is smaller than the S2 envelope.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_1st")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "With a PR interval of 280 ms, S1 at the apex is:",
                choices: ["Soft", "Loud", "Split widely", "Unchanged"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "A long PR leaves time for the leaflets to drift towards each other before the ventricle contracts, so they close from a short distance and S1 is soft.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Complete block: S1 changes from beat to beat",
            markdown: r#"
In **complete heart block** the atria and the ventricles beat independently. Each QRS finds the
atrium at a different moment, so the PR changes from beat to beat and **S1 varies in loudness**,
with an occasional very loud one when an atrial contraction falls just before the QRS (Felner,
ch. 22; MSD Manual).

Watch the P waves march through the slow escape rhythm and compare each S1 on the lane with the PR
before it.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_3rd_junctional")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Why does S1 vary in complete heart block?",
                choices: [
                    "The PR interval changes from beat to beat",
                    "The bundle branches block in turn",
                    "The patient breathes",
                    "The valves are diseased",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "With the atria and ventricles dissociated, every beat has its own PR, and the PR sets the loudness of S1.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Left bundle-branch block reverses the split",
            markdown: r#"
**Left bundle-branch block** delays the left ventricle, so the aortic valve closes late: **A2
follows P2**. Inspiration still delays P2, which now moves *towards* A2, so the split is heard in
**expiration** and narrows on inspiration: a **paradoxical** (reversed) split (Felner, ch. 23).

The patient is holding the breath at **end-expiration**, where the reversed split is widest. Open
the **Display** popup, under **Breathing**, and switch to **peak inspiration**: the two sounds come together. The lane
writes the components in the order heard, **P2·A2**.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("lbbb")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Pulmonic, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
                Sim(BreathHold(phase: Some(EndExpiration))),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "In LBBB, the S2 split is:",
                choices: [
                    "Widest in expiration and narrower in inspiration",
                    "Widest in inspiration, like a normal split",
                    "Fixed, the same in both phases",
                    "Absent",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "With A2 late, P2 comes first. Inspiration delays P2 towards A2 and narrows the gap: the reverse of the normal pattern.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Atrial fibrillation: no S4, a changing S1",
            markdown: r#"
In **atrial fibrillation** the atria do not contract. Two things follow for the sounds: the RR
interval is irregular, so **S1 changes in loudness** with the length of each cycle, and there is
**never an S4**, because S4 needs an atrial contraction (Williams, ch. 25).

Listen at the apex and compare the short and the long cycles on the strip.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("atrial_fibrillation")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Bell)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which extra sound can never be heard in atrial fibrillation?",
                choices: ["S4", "S3", "An ejection click", "An opening snap"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "S4 is the sound of a ventricle receiving the atrial kick. Fibrillating atria do not contract, so there is no S4.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "S3: early in diastole",
            markdown: r#"
A **third heart sound** is a low, dull sound **early in diastole**, about 0.12 to 0.16 s after A2,
as blood rushes into a ventricle that is full or failing (Felner, ch. 23; MSD Manual). It is low,
so listen with the **bell**, lightly applied, at the apex. The rhythm "S1 - S2-S3" is the
**ventricular gallop**.

Heart failure with an S3 is loaded. On the lane, find the **S3** label after each S2.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("heart_failure_s3")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Bell)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Where is S3 heard in the cycle?",
                choices: ["Early in diastole, after S2", "Just before S1", "In mid-systole", "With the QRS"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "S3 comes after S2, during rapid ventricular filling. A sound just before S1 would be an S4.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "S4: just before S1",
            markdown: r#"
A **fourth heart sound** is a low sound **just before S1**, produced as the atrium contracts into a
**stiff ventricle** (hypertrophy, hypertension, ischaemia; Williams, ch. 25). It follows the P wave,
so on the strip it lies after the P and before the QRS. Bell, at the apex. The rhythm "S4-S1 - S2"
is the **atrial gallop**. When the heart is fast, S3 and S4 can merge into one loud **summation
gallop**.

Left ventricular hypertrophy with an S4 is loaded. Then try the **Sound Trainer** (Practise), where
every finding of this tutorial loops on its own with a listen counter: repetition is what builds
the skill.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("lvh_s4")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Bell)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "On the strip, an S4 lies:",
                choices: ["After the P wave, before the QRS", "After the T wave", "On the ST segment", "Before the P wave"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "S4 is the atrial contraction filling a stiff ventricle: after the P wave, just before the QRS and S1.",
            )),
        ),
    ],
)
